  "body": "PR2491 leaves open whether a unitary centre-sharing action could work before its nonunitary precondition. This closes that specific escape for the selected word: all 7,400 precondition gates write only to the 112 Y fields, and the full centre reader annihilates those fields. The entire physical reader is therefore unchanged at all 7,401 cuts.\n\nThe proof uses the simple implication `M(Y)=0` and `range(C-I) subset Y` \u21d2 `MC=M`. It needs neither unitary preconditioning nor commutation with entry adapters. The same Gram diagonal `(150,130,146,134,122,126,142,146)` excludes all 1,260 paired-four-cycle output permutations at every audited cut, even with arbitrary unitary input mixing and unitary payload maps on output edges. All 704 inputs, including 480 dirty helpers, remain arbitrary.\n\nThis is a finite cut census plus a written operator lemma under PR2491's inherited exact native-frame binding. It is not a new native compiler audit, a universal changed-cut obstruction, a supplier, a recursive cost saving or a new kappa. Changed totals, later reader-active cuts, output normalization and paid nonunitary actions remain open; PR2500's separate child-cost correspondence obligation still applies.\n\nValidation:\n\n- Registered optimized Python 3.12 replay passes the primary check, separate integer-dyadic forward checker, and five behavioral test groups.\n- Reverse Fraction covectors and forward integer-dyadic execution agree on all 5,632 reader coefficients at all 7,401 cuts. The independent checker imports no predecessor or primary executable.\n- Actual reader-visible writes, source corruption before executable import, missing/typed-corrupted evidence and dedicated guard-removal sensitivity are detected. Reader-preserving prefix sign/order changes correctly pass. A noncommuting fixture tests signs/order, and a generic nonunitary-prefix counterexample prevents overgeneralization.\n- Git-free read-only replay and exact exclusive regeneration pass. Python 3.14 optimized replays and selected-record integrity pass. No hosted CI success, human/formal-kernel review or full selected cold replay is claimed.\n\nReproduce:\n\n```sh\npython3.12 -I -B scripts/research_package.py --package research/selected-reader-precondition-cuts\n```\n\nOnly `research/selected-reader-precondition-cuts/` is added. Base and refreshed canonical main: `0fdd7d82de03e43c9e5a1000dfbcd380d1b687d2`, priority revision 4. PR2491 is pinned at `c25a4ce22a887daf8cba8b3cfad72796752ab2ee` and its head is unchanged at submission. Exact archive/member/source pins, the scope change from the task-picker suggestion, and remaining obligations are in the package.\n\nCanonical claim: https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/2488#discussioncomment-18950286\n\nCredit ywz65/PR2491 for the full reader, typed native binding, obstruction and changed-cut handoff; Aaron Wang/PR2464; Cinna Davis's typed echo; PR2500's cost distinction; Rohan Arun, Jeremy Kothe, Sagar Thakrar and the preserved selected geometry/compiler lineage. New work by Ross Richardson with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance, including separate mathematical verification. Apache-2.0; frozen predecessor files and selected records are unchanged.\n\nSubmission overlap: all 735 open PRs including drafts were paginated at 13:52 UTC. The full Discussion refresh hit an HTTP403 API rate limit, so its last complete snapshot is 1,209 Discussion records at 13:40 UTC. Closed work is covered only by local notes/ledger; arbitrary diffs are not searched. This is bounded overlap evidence, not a novelty certificate.\n",
